From: Gilly <gilroyscott1970@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: restart the NPU on a full chip reset

The L1 SER path stops the NPU, re-hands it the new rings once DMA is back, 
and keeps NAPI off the NPU-owned RRO and TX-free queues. A full chip reset 
(WM/WA watchdog) takes the mt7996_mac_full_reset() path instead, which 
restarts WED but has no NPU equivalent. So on an NPU board the NPU is never 
stopped and never learns the ring addresses recreated by the WFSYS reset, 
and since host TX also runs through the NPU rings, Wi-Fi stays dead until 
reboot. Worse, the NAPI loops only skip RRO queues when WED is active, so 
under NPU napi_disable() runs on queues that were allocated but never had 
NAPI added, and the dereference of their NULL device pointer oopses. 

Mirror the L1 path: stop the NPU at the start of the full reset, apply the 
same queue skips to both NAPI loops, and re-initialise the NPU right after 
the WED restart, failing the restart on error so it is retried rather than 
left with host TX dead. Found by auditing the WED-only paths for missing 
NPU handling; not yet exercised on-device, as full chip resets are rare.

Signed-off-by: Gilly <gilroyscott1970@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/mt7996/mac.c b/mt7996/mac.c
index d687a76b..0cadeb2f 100644
--- a/mt7996/mac.c
+++ b/mt7996/mac.c
@@ -2328,6 +2328,13 @@ mt7996_mac_restart(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
 		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
 			continue;
 
+		if (mt76_npu_device_active(mdev) &&
+		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
+		if (mt76_queue_is_npu_txfree(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
 		if (mdev->q_rx[i].ndesc)
 			napi_disable(&dev->mt76.napi[i]);
 	}
@@ -2344,6 +2351,13 @@ mt7996_mac_restart(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
 		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
 			continue;
 
+		if (mt76_npu_device_active(mdev) &&
+		    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
+		if (mt76_queue_is_npu_txfree(&mdev->q_rx[i]))
+			continue;
+
 		if (mdev->q_rx[i].ndesc) {
 			napi_enable(&dev->mt76.napi[i]);
 			local_bh_disable();
@@ -2397,6 +2411,17 @@ mt7996_mac_restart(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
 				     MT_INT_TX_RX_DONE_EXT);
 	}
 
+	/* The NPU still holds the ring and descriptor addresses from before
+	 * the reset. Hand it the new ones, as the L1 recovery path does. Host
+	 * TX runs through the NPU rings too, so treat a failure like any other
+	 * init step and let mt7996_mac_full_reset() retry the restart.
+	 */
+	ret = __mt7996_npu_hw_init(dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(mdev->dev, "NPU re-init after full reset failed: %d\n", ret);
+		goto out;
+	}
+
 	/* set the necessary init items */
 	ret = mt7996_mcu_set_eeprom(dev);
 	if (ret)
@@ -2475,6 +2500,11 @@ mt7996_mac_full_reset(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
 
 	dev->recovery.hw_full_reset = true;
 
+	/* Quiesce the NPU before the chip is reset, as the L1 recovery path
+	 * does. mt7996_mac_restart() hands it the new rings afterwards.
+	 */
+	mt7996_npu_hw_stop(dev);
+
 	set_bit(MT76_RESTART, &dev->mphy.state);
 	set_bit(MT76_MCU_RESET, &dev->mphy.state);
 	wake_up(&dev->mt76.mcu.wait);
